<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">talk</ansi>

The <ansi fg="command">talk</ansi> command starts a conversation with someone.
They answer you, and a numbered list of things you can say to them
comes up under their words.

<ansi fg="yellow">Usage: </ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">talk</ansi> <ansi fg="cyan"><name></ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">talk to</ansi> <ansi fg="cyan"><name></ansi>

<ansi fg="yellow">Choosing from the list: </ansi>

  Type the number of a line, then press Enter, to say it. They answer,
  and the list comes back so you can ask more. To leave, press Enter on
  its own or choose the last line. Typing any other command also ends
  the conversation, and the game carries out that command.

<ansi fg="yellow">Marks: </ansi>

  A mark beside a name, or beside a line in the list, tells you why it
  is worth your time:

  <ansi fg="yellow">[!]</ansi>  a quest to offer. Choose it to hear what they want, then
       choose Accept to take the quest, or Leave.
  <ansi fg="yellow">[?]</ansi>  a quest you can finish with them now.
  <ansi fg="yellow">[*]</ansi>  something worth hearing.

  A line you have already heard shows in grey.

<ansi fg="yellow">Handing things over: </ansi>

  When someone asks you for an item, give it to them with
  <ansi fg="command">give</ansi> <ansi fg="cyan"><item> <name></ansi>.

<ansi fg="yellow">Examples: </ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">talk saris</ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">talk to korvath</ansi>

<ansi fg="yellow">See Also: </ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">help give</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help quests</ansi>
